.TH ISGREATER 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
isgreater, isgreaterequal, isless, islessequal, islessgreater,
isunordered, iseqsig \- quiet floating-point comparison macros
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, \-lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "int isgreater(" x ", " y );
.br
.BI "int isgreaterequal(" x ", " y );
.br
.BI "int isless(" x ", " y );
.br
.BI "int islessequal(" x ", " y );
.br
.BI "int islessgreater(" x ", " y );
.br
.BI "int isunordered(" x ", " y );
.PP
.BI "int iseqsig(" x ", " y );
.PP
.I x
and
.I y
must be of real floating type
.RB ( float ,
.BR double ,
or
.BR "long double" ).
The macro selects the underlying implementation by usual arithmetic
conversion of
.I x
and
.IR y .
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR isgreater ()
family of macros provides the six IEEE 754 ordered relational
operations as
.B quiet
comparisons: when either operand is NaN the predicate evaluates as
"false" (or, in the case of
.BR isunordered (),
"true") without raising the
.B FE_INVALID
floating-point exception.
.PP
The plain C operators
.BR < ,
.BR <= ,
.BR > ,
and
.BR >=
are
.I signaling
comparisons \(em they raise
.B FE_INVALID
on any NaN operand, including a quiet NaN.  Code that may legitimately
encounter NaN inputs (statistics over data that contain missing values,
graphics culling against degenerate triangles, etc.) should prefer
these macros to the bare operators in order to avoid spurious
exception flags.
.PP
The semantics are summarised below; in every row, the predicate is
.B 0
whenever
.B "isunordered(x, y)"
is true:
.TS
allbox;
lb lb
l l.
Macro	Equivalent ordered relation
.BR isgreater( x , " " y )	x > y
.BR isgreaterequal( x , " " y )	x >= y
.BR isless( x , " " y )	x < y
.BR islessequal( x , " " y )	x <= y
.BR islessgreater( x , " " y )	x < y || x > y
.BR isunordered( x , " " y )	isnan(x) || isnan(y)
.TE
.PP
.BR islessgreater ()
is the strict-inequality test that, unlike
.BR != ,
returns
.B 0
when either operand is NaN.  This matches the IEEE 754
.B "less or greater"
predicate.
.PP
.BR isunordered ()
is the only macro in the family that returns
.B 1
on a NaN operand.
.SS C23: iseqsig
.BR iseqsig ()
is the
.I signaling
counterpart of
.BR == :
it returns
.B 1
iff
.I x
and
.I y
are numerically equal, and
.B 0
otherwise.  Unlike the bare
.B ==
operator (which on x86 with FUCOMI/UCOMISS only signals on a
.I signaling
NaN), and unlike the macros above (which never signal),
.BR iseqsig ()
raises
.B FE_INVALID
whenever either operand is NaN \(em including a quiet NaN.  This is the
predicate to use when NaN must be reported as a domain error.  Added
in C23 §7.12.14.7.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Each macro returns a non-zero
.B int
when its predicate is true, and zero otherwise.  The result has type
.BR int ,
not the floating type of the operands, and may be used directly in
control-flow constructs.
.PP
.BR iseqsig ()
returns
.B 0
on a NaN operand
.I after
raising
.BR FE_INVALID .
.SH ERRORS
None of these macros set
.IR errno .
.PP
The first six macros never raise a floating-point exception.
.BR iseqsig ()
raises
.B FE_INVALID
iff either operand is NaN.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR isgreater (),
.BR isgreaterequal (),
.BR isless (),
.BR islessequal (),
.BR islessgreater (),
.BR isunordered ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
T{
.BR iseqsig ()
T}	Thread safety	T{
MT-Safe; raises
.B FE_INVALID
on NaN
T}
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR isgreater (),
.BR isgreaterequal (),
.BR isless (),
.BR islessequal (),
.BR islessgreater (),
and
.BR isunordered ()
are specified by C99 §7.12.14 and POSIX.1-2008.  Their non-signaling
behaviour was made mandatory in C99 Annex F.3 (when
.B __STDC_IEC_559__
is defined).
.PP
.BR iseqsig ()
was added in C23 §7.12.14.7 and corresponds to the IEEE 754-2008
.B compareSignalingEqual
operation.
.SH EXAMPLES
The standard idiom is to use the macro form whenever a NaN operand is
possible and you do not want
.B FE_INVALID
recorded:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <fenv.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double a[] = { 1.0, 2.0, NAN, 3.0, NAN, 4.0 };
    int n = sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0]);
    double max = a[0];

    feclearexcept(FE_INVALID);
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        /*
         * The bare \(dq>\(dq operator would raise FE_INVALID on every
         * NaN element of the array, even though we treat them as
         * "skip this element".  isgreater() compares quietly.
         */
        if (isgreater(a[i], max))
            max = a[i];
    }
    printf("max = %g  (FE_INVALID = %d)\en",
           max, fetestexcept(FE_INVALID) != 0);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
max = 4  (FE_INVALID = 0)
.EE
.RE
.PP
By contrast, replacing
.BR isgreater ()
with the bare
.B >
operator yields
.B FE_INVALID = 1
because the NaN comparisons signal.
.PP
When NaN
.I should
be reported as a domain error \(em e.g. validating user input that may
have come from
.BR strtod ()
\(em use
.BR iseqsig ():
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <fenv.h>

int
in_range(double x, double lo, double hi)
{
    feclearexcept(FE_INVALID);
    /* iseqsig signals on NaN x; caller can detect via fetestexcept. */
    if (iseqsig(x, x))
        return x >= lo && x <= hi;
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
